        {
          "type": "output_text",
          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"bronte-jane-eyre-091b963d938b794f\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":1,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":0,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.96,\"analysis\":\"Jane’s embedded “I love you,” as quoted back by Rochester, is principally a considered avowal of her loving state and an explanation for her hope of living with him. The betrothal and shared-future setting gives it slight commitment resonance, but the words do not independently undertake a new obligation. Its earnest and emotional delivery does not show reflex-like expressive discharge.\",\"ambiguity\":\"The highlighted token is Rochester’s immediate revoicing of Jane’s earlier direct statement; the scores characterize the original love avowal represented by that quotation.\"},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"quoted_or_revoiced\",\"description\":\"Rochester quotes Jane’s statement back to her shortly after she originally uttered it; the quotation preserves and foregrounds her original avowal.\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"Jane originally gives the avowal calmly within a reflective account of her happiness, thoughts of Rochester, and anticipated life with him. Rochester then repeats it, asks her to reaffirm it, and treats her subsequent confirmation as marked by “faith, truth, and devotion.” The solemnity, tenderness, and relational setting strengthen the sincerity and intensity of the avowal, but do not independently establish expressive/reflexive force; the impending marriage supplies only modest performative resonance.\",\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"e1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Jane’s original statement is presented in the wider passage as: “I think it a glorious thing to have the hope of living with you, because I love you.”\",\"supports\":\"The love clause explicitly presents Jane’s loving state as true and as the reason for her hope of a shared life, strongly supporting T.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Rochester revoices the statement—“because I love you”—and asks, “Do you love me, Jane?—repeat it.”\",\"supports\":\"The highlighted occurrence is a quotation or revoicing of Jane’s prior avowal rather than a new first-person declaration by Rochester.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Jane answers, “I do, sir—I do, with my whole heart.”\",\"supports\":\"Her immediate reaffirmation confirms the avowal reading and its presented sincerity.\",\"confidence\":0.98},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Rochester says she spoke with “earnest, religious energy” and describes her gaze as expressing “faith, truth, and devotion.”\",\"supports\":\"This supports emotional seriousness and some relational commitment resonance, while providing no evidence that the words were a reflex-like discharge.\",\"confidence\":0.94},{\"evidence_id\":\"e5\",\"source\":\"supplied_metadata\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The occurrence is identified as an affirmative present-tense explicit love expression in Chapter XXV of Charlotte Brontë’s Jane Eyre.\",\"supports\":\"Confirms the textual identity and grammatical form of the classified occurrence.\",\"confidence\":1.0}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T/P/E gives a natural account: the utterance is dominantly truth-conditional, with slight commitment resonance from the anticipated shared life, and no independent evidence for expressive/reflexive force. No important aspect of the core force falls outside T/P/E.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":null}"
